After chunk-6 (this file)
-------------------------
42 of 42 photos byte-identical at default-on across both distances and efforts.
Net wins: 0 (no observed real-content wins under the chunk-5 discriminator).
Net regressions: 0 (was 10 cells across 4 images, up to +1.19%).

Chunk-5 corpus regression (3 synthetics + 5 CLIC photos + 3 gb82-sc
screenshots × e7/e8/e9) re-verified BYTE-IDENTICAL: see chunk-5 bench
above. The chunk-6 bbox-span gate fires only on the discriminator-
bypass path (non-screenshot images); the chunk-5 corpus is dominated
by either flat-bg synthetics (discriminator skips) or photos where
the chunk-4 cost gate already rejects every candidate.

Hash-locks: `hash_lock_features` 36/36 byte-identical (auto_splines
defaults remain off, so no impact on the default encode path).

Test impact
-----------
6 of 6 `tests/auto_splines.rs` integration tests pass unchanged.
The `auto_splines_power_line_changes_bitstream` test image
(1024×256 stripe+ramp bg + bright horizontal wire) has wire bbox
(0, 115, 1024, 142) = span 1024 = exactly image long dim, so the
spline candidate clears the chunk-6 gate. The test continues to
assert `bytes_off != bytes_on` (admission). No test expectation
was relaxed.

30 of 30 splines lib tests pass (`cargo test -p jxl-encoder splines`).
RD-regression (18 cells, 6 photos × 3 distances) all within thresholds:
size < 103% of baseline, butteraugli < 105% of baseline, ssim2 > baseline - 1.0.

Default-on candidacy
--------------------
    e7  NO  (no observed wins; chunk-6 only closes risk for opt-in callers)
    e8  NO  (same)
    e9  NO  (same)

The chunk-5 audit concluded auto_splines provides no observed wins on
real content. Chunk-6 strengthens the trial-encode gate against a
specific failure mode (long-ridge segments riding through textured
photo content) but doesn't change the no-wins conclusion. Flipping
the default still adds a per-encode `compute_mask1x1` pass for zero
observable benefit.

